          "On the other axle: CV boots age at similar rates because they were installed at the same time and exposed to the same conditions. When one boot has torn, the other is typically near the same condition. We inspect the opposite axle on the lift not to upsell but because doing both at once costs less than a return visit a few months later when the second side fails.",
          "The cost progression here is steep. A boot replacement caught before the grease escapes is the cheapest intervention. A complete CV axle replacement at the clicking stage is straightforward. A joint that fails completely while driving can damage the wheel hub, ABS sensor, or brake components — turning a single-axle replacement into a multi-component repair."
